course: dictionary
lesson: 6973
title: Meet the pangolin
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pangolin is a mammal, like us, but its body is covered in hard scales instead of fur. «The pangolin is covered in hard overlapping scales made of keratin, like fingernails.» Overlapping means the scales lie over each other, like roof tiles.
explain: Pangolins live in Africa and Asia. They have no teeth, so they catch ants and termites with a very long, sticky tongue and lick them up.
explain: When a pangolin is scared, it rolls up into a tight ball, so its hard scales protect it. Scientists check which animals are related by their genes and bodies, not by how they look, because looks can fool us.
fact: A pangolin is a mammal covered in hard, overlapping scales.
fact: Pangolins eat ants and termites, using a long, sticky tongue.
fact: When scared, a pangolin rolls up into a tight ball.
fact: There are eight kinds of pangolin: four in Asia and four in Africa.
fact: Pangolins are among the most smuggled mammals, because people illegally hunt them for their scales and meat.
exercise: What kind of animal is a pangolin? | A bird with feathers | A fish that lives in the sea | A plant covered in leaves | *A mammal covered in hard scales
exercise: What does a pangolin do when it is scared? | It dives under the sea | It flies up into the trees | It sings loudly | *It rolls up into a tight ball
exercise: «The pangolin has a very long, sticky tongue to lick up ants.» What does a pangolin eat? | Fish and frogs | Grass and leaves | Apples and nuts | *Ants and termites
test: What are pangolin scales made of? | Glass, like a window | Metal, like a coin | *Keratin, like fingernails | Bone, like a skull
test: Where do pangolins live? | Australia and Antarctica | Only on islands in the sea | *Africa and Asia | Europe and South America
exam: «Looks can also fool us.» How do scientists decide which animals are related? | By their colour | By their size | *By their genes and bodies | By how they look
